//
//! The build half of GMP's runtime route: four modules and two generated files.
//!
//! An SDK that ships the runtime route calls `createModule` in place of
//! `translate.createModule()` and imports what comes back as its `c` module.
//! Nothing here links libgmp. That is the point: in this configuration the
//! symbols arrive through the platform loader at run time, and linking the
//! archive as well would make the linker demand them before `load` ever runs.
//!
//!   rt        runtime/loader.zig, the reusable core. Pure Zig; imported by the
//!             facade in both configurations, because `LoadSource` and
//!             `LoadError` are this SDK's public types and must be the same
//!             types whichever configuration is built.
//!   library   runtime/library.zig, this SDK's descriptor: the namespaced
//!             synchronization binding, the one load-time check, and the ABI
//!             statement the check rests on.
//!   dispatch  generated by runtime/generate.zig from the real translation
//!             output of `include/gmp_all.h`: the table, the wrappers, `load`,
//!             and the re-exported translated namespace. It imports `rt`,
//!             `library` and the patched translation as `c_native`.
//!   native    the generator's second file: the same translation with the
//!             `__root` prologue Aro writes replaced by an import of the
//!             dispatch as `c_routed`, so a declaration nested inside a
//!             translated type - the `__gmp_randstate_struct` members - names
//!             the routed wrapper rather than the raw extern. It imports the
//!             dispatch and nothing else.
//!   sync.c    compiled into the dispatch with `-DRT_SYNC_PREFIX=gmp_rt`, so
//!             its symbols cannot collide with another SDK's.
//!
//! ## The one thing a caller must not do
//!
//! Do not link the compiled C archive into a module that imports this
//! dispatch. The facade's `c` import is the dispatch, and nothing else in the
//! graph should reference the library, or the linker resolves the symbols
//! statically and the runtime route silently becomes a linked one.
//!
//! ## What this SDK's translated namespace contains
//!
//! `include/gmp_all.h` pulls in `<stdio.h>` and `<stdarg.h>` before `gmp.h`,
//! because `gmp.h` declares its whole `gmp_fprintf` / `gmp_vsnprintf` /
//! `gmp_sscanf` family only behind `FILE` and `va_list`. That is what makes the
//! printf and scanf entry points part of the public surface at all, and it is
//! also what drags the C library's own stdio surface into the same translation.
//! The two are told apart by name, and the rule is short enough to state:
//!
//!   * every symbol GMP exports starts with `__gmp`. The header reaches that
//!     spelling through macro expansion. `#define mpz_init __gmpz_init` renames
//!     the declaration. `#define mpn_add __MPN(add)` renames it again through
//!     `__MPN`. `#define mpz_kronecker mpz_jacobi` chains onto another
//!     declaration. Expanding the 370 declarations in the generated header
//!     `zig-out/include/gmp.h` gives 294 distinct symbols. All of them are
//!     `__gmp*` (`SOURCE-CONFIRMED`, 2026-10-04).
//!   * no C library symbol is spelled that way. Not one of the 575 names in
//!     `libc_noise` below begins with `__gmp`, on any of the C libraries this
//!     build has translated.
//!
//! ## What the dispatch must do with an alias
//!
//! `translate-c` turns each of those `#define`s into a declaration of its own:
//! `pub const mpz_init = __gmpz_init;`. The alias must name the declaration in
//! the generated dispatch, which is the routed wrapper. It must not re-export
//! `c_native.mpz_init`, which is the raw extern: that would hand every facade
//! call straight to the linker, and in a runtime-loading build nothing links
//! libgmp, so the program would not link at all. Aliases chain, so
//! `mpz_kronecker` names `mpz_jacobi`, which names `__gmpz_jacobi`, and both
//! hops stay inside the dispatch. That is the whole requirement, and this SDK
//! cannot be loaded without it.
//!
//! The three exported objects need the same care. `pub const gmp_version =
//! __gmp_version;` cannot name a value the dispatch does not hold, so a data
//! alias wants its own guarded accessor beside the one its target gets. This
//! package does not depend on that form: it reads the `__gmp_*` spellings
//! (`gmp.providerVersion`, `gmp.providerErrno`, `gmp.providerBitsPerLimb`),
//! which the generator already produces as `<NAME>Addr()`.
//!
//! `libc_noise` spells that rule out by name. It is the union of what those
//! headers declare, read from the pinned compiler's own headers:
//!
//!   * glibc's `generic-glibc/stdio.h`, `bits/stdio-ldbl.h` and
//!     `bits/types/cookie_io_functions_t.h`,
//!   * Darwin's `any-darwin-any/stdio.h`,
//!   * mingw's `any-windows-any/stdio.h` and `corecrt_stdio_config.h`,
//!   * musl's `wasm-wasi-musl/stdio.h` and `bits/alltypes.h`, and
//!   * glibc's `obstack.h` and `string.h`, because `include/gmp_all.h`
//!     includes `<obstack.h>` where the target has one. That is how
//!     `_GMP_H_HAVE_OBSTACK` becomes 1 in the header, and `<obstack.h>` pulls
//!     `<string.h>` in with it: both add declarations that belong to the C
//!     library and are therefore direct. `_obstack_begin`, `_obstack_begin_1`,
//!     `_obstack_memory_used`, `_obstack_newchunk`, `__obstack_free`,
//!     `obstack_alloc_failed_handler` and `obstack_exit_failure` come from that
//!     one include, and only on the targets where it applies.
//!
//!   * glibc's `_FORTIFY_SOURCE` helpers, which the same two headers declare
//!     only when `_FORTIFY_SOURCE` is defined. The pinned translator defines
//!     it for exactly one of the four optimize modes this build supports -
//!     `-Doptimize=ReleaseSafe`, which it passes on as `-O2` - and declares
//!     none of these in Debug, ReleaseFast or ReleaseSmall (measured from the
//!     four generated dispatches, 2026-10-04). They are the C library's in
//!     every case: the twelve `__*_chk` entry points are exported by `libc.so.6`,
//!     and the five `__*_alias` and three `__*_chk_warn` names are not symbols
//!     at all. glibc declares each of those eight through `__REDIRECT`, whose
//!     `__asm__` label names the unfortified function
//!     (`bits/stdio2-decl.h:96` gives `__fgets_alias` the label `fgets`), and the
//!     translator keeps the declaration name while dropping the label, so a C
//!     compiler never emits a reference under that name either. Asking a GMP
//!     provider for them is therefore unsatisfiable by construction, and eight of
//!     the twenty fail the load; keeping them direct is what the linked
//!     configuration already does with the same translation.
//!
//! A name in the list that the target's headers do not declare matches nothing
//! and costs nothing. A missing name does not fail quietly: the load reports
//! the symbol by name.
//!
//! ## Where the last rows of that list come from
//!
//! The rows above them were written by hand from the headers. The rows after
//! them were measured: `include/gmp_all.h` was translated with the pinned
//! Zig 0.16.0 for every target in the matrix, and every name that was neither
//! GMP's nor already listed was read out of the output (`SOURCE-CONFIRMED`,
//! 2026-10-04). That added 175 names, from mingw's `stdio.h` and
//! `sec_api/stdio_s.h`, WASI's `wasi/api.h`, glibc's and musl's `stdio.h` and
//! `strings.h`, FreeBSD's `stdio.h` and `sys/types.h`, OpenBSD's `stdio.h`,
//! Darwin's `stdio.h` and `sys/stdio.h`, and mingw's `_mingw.h`.
//!
//! That sweep did not translate every target: the nine NetBSD targets and six
//! OpenBSD ones failed inside `translate-c`, and `hexagon-linux` failed in the
//! GMP header render. Those are recorded as failures, and no name here is
//! claimed for a target whose translation produced none.

const std = @import("std");

/// The prefix `runtime/sync.c` is compiled with.
///
/// `runtime/library.zig` declares the same prefix in the symbol names it binds.
/// The two must agree, and a build where they do not fails to link, which is
/// the whole error report: there is no runtime path that could hide it.
///
/// A build that needs a second, independent instance of this SDK in one binary
/// should change this value *and* the prefix in `runtime/library.zig`, and then
/// give the second instance its own copy of both files. Nothing else in the
/// package assumes one instance.
const sync_prefix = "gmp_rt";

/// `extern` declarations that the C library provides and that must stay direct.
